------------------------------------------- Getting started ----------------------------------------

- LET'S GENERATE THE PROJECT SKELETON, AND BEGIN OUR ADVENTURE!

- All the required files can be found inside the resource1 folder:

- Instructions:

#) Generate the project:

        ng new ponyracer --defaults --no-routing --prefix pr --skip-install

    👉--skip-install does not do 'npm install' the packages in package.json.
    

1️⃣ Replace package.json file in the root of your app (by directly copying the file from files folder),
    to add a few dependencies, especially one called ng2ninja which will be responsible to analyze your
    code and submit your results to the website. and run npm install to fetch the dependencies

        npm install

    If you don't want ng2ninja package:
        
        npm uninstall ng2ninja


2️⃣ The Angular CLI generates a default Prettier configuration file (.prettierrc) when creating a new
    project. Replace it with the one provided in the resources to use our custom formatting rules.


3️⃣ The CLI used to come with e2e tests already configured with Protractor. Since Angular CLI v12, this
    is no longer the case. We need to pick the end-to-end testing library we want, and add it to our
    project.
    We are going to use Playwright. We could set it up by hand, but the easiest way is to use a
    schematic. The Angular CLI is a very powerful tool. It allows third-party developers to build their
    own "extensions" of the CLI called "builders", and their own generators called "schematics".

    This Playwright schematic is a schematic provided by the community that we’re going to use to add
    Playwright to our project:

        ng add playwright-ng-schematics@21.0.9 --install-browsers --skip-confirmation


4️⃣ For a long time, the CLI included TSLint as well out of the box to check our code quality. But
    TSLint is now deprecated, so this is no longer the case since CLI v12. But no worries, we’re going
    to use ESLint instead.

        ng add angular-eslint@21.3.1 --skip-confirmation


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


#) Serve the application:

1️⃣ Update the CLI configuration by replacing the angular.json file with the one provided. Everything is
    pre-configured: the build tool, the dependencies, the tests, and even our first components!
    The project is versioned using Git by default, and uses Vite for the build. Still from the ponyracer
    folder, run the project locally by executing:

        ng serve

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


#) Add some styles:

1️⃣ We will be using bootstrap and fontawesome to style our application.

        npm install bootstrap
        npm install @fortawesome/fontawesome-free


2️⃣ We now need to use these styles in our application. Open the file styles.css and add the following:

    styles.css:

        @import 'bootstrap/dist/css/bootstrap.css';
        @import '@fortawesome/fontawesome-free/css/all.css';
    
    Then, in the template of the root component, app.html, let’s replace the content with:

    app.html:

        <main class="container" style="margin-top: 70px;">
            <h1>Ponyracer</h1>
        </main>
    
    Restart the ng serve task, and your browser should refresh automatically and display our new template.


3️⃣ The TypeScript configuration lives in tsconfig.json, and, as we saw, the compiler will help us to
    write good code. We can add a few options to make the compiler a bit stricter and catch more
    potential mistakes. So let’s replace the file generated by the CLI with the one in the resources.


---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


#) Unit tests:

1️⃣ Let’s customize Vitest by adding a configuration file:

        ng generate config vitest

    Then replace the generated vitest-base.config.ts file in the root of your application with the one
    provided in the resources.


2️⃣ Copy and replace the tsconfig.spec.json file into the root of your application

3️⃣ Copy paste the setup-test.ts file into the src directory. This file configures the Vitest test
    environment: it adds a check to ensure that no console errors occur during tests, and extends Vitest
    locators with a utility method.

4️⃣ Let’s first check if the unit tests are OK. Create a results directory at the root of your
    application. Then replace app.spec.ts in your project (src/app) with the one provided, then run:

        ng test

    👉By default, the tests are in "watch" mode, which means they will re-run on each file change. To
    launch the tests only once, you can run:

        ng test --no-watch

    👉You can also display a UI with the --ui option.

    👉The tests will also check the code coverage of your application. To run the tests with coverage,
    use the --coverage flag:

        ng test --coverage --no-watch

    The coverage results are displayed in the terminal (text summary), and an HTML report is generated
    in the coverage/ directory. Open coverage/ponyracer/index.html in your browser to explore the
    detailed report.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


#) End-to-end tests:

1️⃣ Now let’s see if the end-to-end tests are OK. Copy home.e2e-spec.ts in the e2e directory, and
    remove the default test example.spec.ts.

2️⃣ Playwright has its own configuration file, replace playwright.config.ts (in the root directory) with
    the one provided. Also add the tsconfig.json file in the e2e directory provided in the resources.
    Then launch the tests with:

        ng e2e

    ⚠️On some systems, you may need to run npx playwright install-deps to install missing system
    dependencies. You should have an explicit error message if this is the case.

    👉If you want to have a UI to see the tests running, you can run the tests with:

        ng e2e --ui


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


#) Lint:

1️⃣ Last step, check the code quality. The packages are already installed, we just need to update the
    configuration file. Let’s replace the eslint.config.js file at the root of the project with the
    provided eslint.config.mjs file (note the different extension .mjs) and check the code quality with:

        ng lint

    Fix any error found (if any) to achieve 100%.

    ⚠️Note that you can run:

        ng lint --fix

    at any moment to automatically format your code, and get rid of annoying errors like missing
    whitespaces, semi-colons, etc.ng